{"case_id": "MB_17", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 57-year-old man presents to the emergency department with several days of malaise, weakness, and night sweats. Today, he experienced a headache with blurry vision, thus prompting his presentation. The patient has a history of diabetes and is followed closely by an endocrinologist. Otherwise, he has lost 10 pounds over the past month. His temperature is 100°F (37.8°C), blood pressure is 122/90 mmHg, pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Physical exam is notable for a thin man but is otherwise unremarkable. His visual acuity is 20/100 in both eyes, though he says that he normally has 20/20 vision. His gait is mildly ataxic as well, but his neurological exam is otherwise nonfocal. Laboratory studies are ordered as seen below.\n\nHemoglobin: 10 g/dL\nHematocrit: 31%\nLeukocyte count: 57,500/mm^3 with 35% blasts\nPlatelet count: 109,000/mm^3\n\nSerum:\nNa+: 139 mEq/L\nCl-: 100 mEq/L\nK+: 4.1 Eq/L\nHCO3-: 22 mEq/L\nBUN: 20 mg/dL\nGlucose: 99 mg/dL\nCreatinine: 1.1 mg/dL\nCalcium: 10.0 mEq/L\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia\nB. Acute myelogenous leukemia\nC. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\nD. Chronic myelogenous leukemia\nE. Multiple myeloma\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Systemic Constitutional Syndrome with Neurological Features and Possible Hematological or Inflammatory Aetiology", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["Several days of malaise and weakness with constitutional symptoms (night sweats)", "10-lb weight loss over past month", "Fever (temp 37.8°C)", "Nonfocal neurological exam with mild ataxia and bilateral decreased visual acuity", "Laboratory findings showing pancytopenia (Hb 10 g/dL, pltts 109K) with blasts"], "excluded_candidates": ["Neurological deficit syndrome: less likely due to absence of focal findings (no focal weakness/ataxia or cranial nerve signs)", "Metabolic decompensation (diabetic): less likely given the blasts and constitutional signs rather than acute metabolic derangement", "Infectious myelopathy: possible but pancytopenia with blasts raises higher suspicion for hematological cause first"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional signs (night sweats, fever, weight loss)", "Pancytopenia with blasts", "Nonfocal neurological signs in context of systemic disease"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.3329165100778152, "posterior": 0.3561094098626208, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["35% blasts + pancytopenia with subacute constitutional symptoms strongly suggest acute myeloid leukemia over lymphoid or chronic processes. High blasts are typical for AML but atypical for CML or ALL in this context.", "Lack of chronic leukocytosis/splenomegaly in the vignette argues against chronic myeloproliferative disorders like CML, favoring acute leukemia (AML/ALL) as the presentation is more compatible with immediate bone marrow failure."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Are blasts morphologically myeloid or lymphoid?", "Does the bone marrow biopsy show dysplasia or fibrosis?", "Is there a history of chronic myeloproliferative disease?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Flow cytometry for blast lineage characterization", "Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy", "Cytogenetic analysis (e.g., Philadelphia chromosome testing)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "test_pathway"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.08255839341962438, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No evidence of lymphoid lineage or M-protein effectively excludes lymphoid/plasma cell neoplasms."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.08255839341962438, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of chronic CML markers reduces probability of blast crisis. This strengthens AML as the systemic explanation."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.05869758607291213, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No evidence of lymphoid lineage or M-protein effectively excludes lymphoid/plasma cell neoplasms."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.01772195879688272, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["35% blasts + pancytopenia with subacute constitutional symptoms strongly suggest acute myeloid leukemia over lymphoid or chronic processes. High blasts are typical for AML but atypical for CML or ALL in this context."]}, "B6": {"id": "B6", "label": "Other Mass", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.04975050438624893, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.3004673145715863, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Mild ataxia and nonfocal neurological findings may raise suspicion for posterior fossa mass, but the evidence is weak given the patient's systemic symptoms and blasts."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia (CML) in Blast Crisis", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.02782104764551726, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of chronic CML markers reduces probability of blast crisis. This strengthens AML as the systemic explanation."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS) with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.02782104764551726, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of dysplasia strongly reduces MDS with excess blasts probability. Pancytopenia with blasts is more consistent with de novo AML."]}, "B6.1": {"id": "B6.1", "label": "Posterior Fossa Mass", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.020923109321319643, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "B6", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Subacute presentation without progressive symptoms weakens case for posterior fossa mass in favor of systemic causes with neuro involvement."]}, "B6.2": {"id": "B6.2", "label": "Visual Pathway / Optic Pathway Mass", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.014878655517382858, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B6", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Findings are less typical for central optic pathway lesion; the absence of progressive symptoms argues against a brain mass differential."]}, "B6.3": {"id": "B6.3", "label": "Metastatic Brain Lesion", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.013948739547546427, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B6", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would neuro-ophthalmic findings (blurred vision, nonfocal ataxia) support posterior fossa mass as a differential for this patient’s symptoms?", "result_summary": "Mild ataxia and nonfocal neurological findings may raise suspicion for posterior fossa mass, but the evidence is weak given the patient's systemic symptoms and blasts.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B6.1": "weak_for", "B1": "moderate_against", "B1.1": "moderate_against", "B1.2": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the bilateral visual acuity decline and non-macular ataxia suggest a central optic pathway lesion like a brain mass?", "result_summary": "Findings are less typical for central optic pathway lesion; the absence of progressive symptoms argues against a brain mass differential.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B6.2": "weak_against", "B6.1": "moderate_against", "B1.1": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Lack of prior chronic leukocytosis or splenomegaly in the vignette makes CML in blast crisis less likely than de novo AML.", "result_summary": "Absence of chronic CML markers reduces probability of blast crisis. This strengthens AML as the systemic explanation.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.2": "moderate_against", "B1.1": "moderate_for", "B3": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of pancytopenia with blasts and lack of multilineage dysplasia in findings argue against MDS with excess blasts?", "result_summary": "Absence of dysplasia strongly reduces MDS with excess blasts probability. Pancytopenia with blasts is more consistent with de novo AML.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.3": "moderate_against", "B1.1": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 6, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the described blasts with no mention of lymphoid lineage or M-protein suggest lymphoid/plasma cell disorders are unlikely?", "result_summary": "No evidence of lymphoid lineage or M-protein effectively excludes lymphoid/plasma cell neoplasms.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2": "strong_against", "B4": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 7, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Absence of chronic neurological symptoms like progressive ataxia or cranial nerve deficits argues against posterior fossa mass over systemic disease.", "result_summary": "Subacute presentation without progressive symptoms weakens case for posterior fossa mass in favor of systemic causes with neuro involvement.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B6.1": "moderate_against", "B1.1": "moderate_for"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.3329165100778152, "B2": 0.19758459541203668, "B3": 0.19758459541203668, "B4": 0.1404792210154943, "B5": 0.042413447182688496, "B6": 0.08902163089992862, "B1.1": 0.1997499060466891, "B1.2": 0.06658330201556305, "B1.3": 0.06658330201556305, "B6.1": 0.05007466738120985, "B6.2": 0.022255407724982156, "B6.3": 0.016691555793736615}, {"B1": 0.3561094098626208, "B2": 0.08255839341962438, "B3": 0.08255839341962438, "B4": 0.05869758607291213, "B5": 0.01772195879688272, "B6": 0.04975050438624893, "B1.1": 0.3004673145715863, "B1.2": 0.02782104764551726, "B1.3": 0.02782104764551726, "B6.1": 0.020923109321319643, "B6.2": 0.014878655517382858, "B6.3": 0.013948739547546427}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers or time-critical syndrome patterns require immediate life-saving intervention. Pancytopenia with blasts and non-focal neurological deficits are primarily diagnostic concerns rather than instability syndromes.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1: Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts) has posterior 0.356 < 0.75. Dangerous alternatives B1.2 (CML in blast crisis, danger 0.75) and B1.3 (MDS, danger 0.85) remain unresolved with posteriors ≥0.10. Depth limit not reached yet (current level 1 vs max 3)."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.35, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3004673145715863, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 57-year-old man presents to the emergency department with several days of malaise, weakness, and night sweats. Today, he experienced a headache with blurry vision, thus prompting his presentation. The patient has a history of diabetes and is followed closely by an endocrinologist. Otherwise, he has lost 10 pounds over the past month. His temperature is 100°F (37.8°C), blood pressure is 122/90 mmHg, pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Physical exam is notable for a thin man but is otherwise unremarkable. His visual acuity is 20/100 in both eyes, though he says that he normally has 20/20 vision. His gait is mildly ataxic as well, but his neurological exam is otherwise nonfocal. Laboratory studies are ordered as seen below.\n\nHemoglobin: 10 g/dL\nHematocrit: 31%\nLeukocyte count: 57,500/mm^3 with 35% blasts\nPlatelet count: 109,000/mm^3\n\nSerum:\nNa+: 139 mEq/L\nCl-: 100 mEq/L\nK+: 4.1 Eq/L\nHCO3-: 22 mEq/L\nBUN: 20 mg/dL\nGlucose: 99 mg/dL\nCreatinine: 1.1 mg/dL\nCalcium: 10.0 mEq/L", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Age: 57-year-old man", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: malaise, weakness, and night sweats for several days", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Additional symptom: headache with blurry vision today", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Past medical history: diabetes mellitus", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Patient monitored by: endocrinologist (close follow-up)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Weight loss: 10 lb (4.5 kg) over past month", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Temperature: 100.0°F (37.8°C)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Blood pressure: 122/90 mmHg", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pulse: 84/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Respirations: 16/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Oxygen saturation: 99% on room air", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E12", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam finding: thin but otherwise unremarkable", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E13", "kind": "direct", "content": "Visual acuity: 20/100 in both eyes (previously 20/20)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E14", "kind": "direct", "content": "Gait finding: mildly ataxic", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E15", "kind": "direct", "content": "Neurological exam: otherwise nonfocal", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E16", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: hemoglobin 10 g/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E17", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: hematocrit 31%", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E18", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: leukocyte count 57,500/mm³", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E19", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: 35% blasts on leukocyte count", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E20", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: platelet count 109,000/mm³", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E21", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: sodium 139 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E22", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: chloride 100 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E23", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: potassium 4.1 Eq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E24", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: bicarbonate 22 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E25", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: BUN 20 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E26", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: glucose 99 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E27", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: creatinine 1.1 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E28", "kind": "direct", "content": "Laboratory finding: calcium 10.0 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Enlarged posterior fossa', 'Multiple myeloma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=86%, source=both]\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia (CML) in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS) with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: myelodysplasia, somatic mutation\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Metastatic Brain Lesion:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: changes in breathing rate, changes in pulse rate, confusion, difficulty speaking, double vision\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, somatic mutation\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia (CML) in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS) with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: myelodysplasia, somatic mutation\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Metastatic Brain Lesion:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: changes in breathing rate, changes in pulse rate, confusion, difficulty speaking, double vision\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia (CML) in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS) with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: myelodysplasia\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Metastatic Brain Lesion:\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: changes in breathing rate, changes in pulse rate, confusion, difficulty speaking, double vision\n\nChronic Myelogenous Leukemia (CML) in Blast Crisis vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS) with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: myelodysplasia"}
