{"case_id": "MB_9", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue. In general, he has been in good health; however, he recently has experienced weight loss, abdominal pain, and general fatigue. He has a medical history of anxiety, diabetes, a fracture of his foot sustained when he tripped, and a recent cold that caused him to miss work for 1 week. His current medications include metformin, insulin, buspirone, vitamin D, calcium, and sodium docusate. His temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 150/100 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals a calm gentleman. A mild systolic murmur is heard in the left upper sternal region. The rest of the physical exam is within normal limits. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 12 g/dL Hematocrit: 36% Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm^3 with normal differential Platelet count: 177,000/mm^3 Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L K+: 4.3 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 120 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.9 mEq/L AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma\nB. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\nC. Chronic myeloid leukemia\nD. Leukemoid reaction\nE. Multiple myeloma\n", "root": {"label": "Chronic Progressive Constitutional Syndrome with Elevated Leukocyte Count (Reactive vs. Neoplastic Differentiation) and Hypercalcemia", "time_course": "unspecified", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["Chief complaint: fatigue", "Secondary symptoms: unintentional weight loss, abdominal pain", "Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm^3 with normal differential", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase", "Serum calcium (Ca2+): 10.9 mEq/L (elevated)", "Medical history: diabetes mellitus, recent viral infection"], "excluded_candidates": ["Chronic myeloid leukemia (ruled out by elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase and normal differential in context)", "Viral-induced reactive lymphocytosis (lower priority due to constitutional weight loss and hypercalcemia)", "Neoplastic bone marrow infiltration (plausible given abnormal WBC count but requires further investigation)"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Elevated serum calcium", "Significant leukocytosis"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5488441461595824, "posterior": 0.4971381847914963, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Hypercalcemia in this context (elevated LAK and normal differential) is more indicative of a plasma cell disorder like multiple myeloma (B1), which commonly presents with bone destruction and elevated calcium.", "Hypercalcemia is rarely caused by leukemoid reactions (B2). This finding is more directly compatible with lymphoproliferative/plasma cell diseases (B1) such as multiple myeloma."], "evidence_against": ["A normal leukocyte differential is less specific for plasma cell disorders (B1) than for myeloproliferative neoplasms. The elevated LAK (suggestive of CML) was already used to park B3, making this finding neutral for B1."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly?", "Has the patient had bone pain or pathologic fractures?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Flow cytometry (CD5/CD19 staining)", "Bone marrow biopsy for plasmacytosis"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Leukemoid Reaction (Reactive Leukocytosis)", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.034069228672662835, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Hypercalcemia is far more typical of plasma cell disorders (B1.1) than leukemoid reactions (B2), but without specific LR data, this remains weak evidence."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.019468130670093054, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.01168087840205583, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.00778725226803722, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Multiple Myeloma", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.40369115757504964, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of bone pain/fractures may reflect early-stage disease or variable presentation in myeloma, making this a minor reduction in likelihood."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Other Lymphoproliferative/Plasma Cell Disorders (e.g., MGUS, Plasmacytoma, Lymphocytic Leukemia)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.09344702721644667, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of elevated serum calcium and constitutional symptoms (fatigue, weight loss) align with multiple myeloma's characteristic presentation of bone marrow infiltration and hypercalcemia?", "result_summary": "Elevated calcium and constitutional symptoms are classic for multiple myeloma (B1.1), but lack of bone-related symptoms introduces uncertainty.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B1.1": "weak_for", "B2": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the documented normal leukocyte differential and elevated LAK in context strongly support leukemoid reaction over lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma?", "result_summary": "Normal differential supports reactive over neoplastic WBC elevation (B2) when combined with elevated LAK (excluding CML), but this finding is less conclusive for plasma cell disorders.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B1.1": "neutral", "B2": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of documented bone pain/lytic pathologic fractures despite hypercalcemia inconsistent with multiple myeloma (B1.1) as the dominant hypothesis in this vignette?", "result_summary": "Lack of bone pain/lytic fractures in myeloma could indicate early disease or alternative mechanisms, but remains plausible in context.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B1.1": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the presence of hypercalcemia inconsistent with leukemoid reaction (B2) given that plasma cell disorders (like multiple myeloma - B1.1) are far more commonly associated with this paraneoplastic finding?", "result_summary": "Hypercalcemia is far more typical of plasma cell disorders (B1.1) than leukemoid reactions (B2), but without specific LR data, this remains weak evidence.", "per_action_branch_effects": 